    "content": "All 13 checklist items have been executed and recorded — 11 PASS, 2 FAIL.\n\n**Summary of the two failures:**\n\n- **IX-12 (360° rotation)** — The panorama tracks rotation as an unbounded value with no modulo-360 wrap. Hotspots render only within a narrow band near rotation 0°; past roughly 36° they disappear and never return, including at exactly 360°, which is visually the identical view to 0° yet renders zero hotspots. Dragging back down to 0° restores them, confirming the azimuth calculation is not wrapped. Zoom behavior on its own was fine — zooming out restored all 8 hotspots.\n\n- **CT-11 (image relevance)** — Unsplash `photo-1513519245088-0e12902e35a6` is assigned to two unrelated items (春节's lead image and 苏绣's gallery image 2), so it cannot be consistent with both; it also fails to load in both places, leaving 春节 without a rendering lead image. Several galleries additionally use generic non-Chinese stock photography unrelated to the named craft.\n\nTwo notes on methodology worth flagging. First, hotspots are in continuous slow auto-rotation, so Playwright's click never found them \"stable\" and timed out; I fell back to dispatching clicks and synthetic mouse drag sequences via `browser_evaluate`. This exercised the app's real handlers, but it is not identical to genuine user input, so the rotation findings would be worth a manual confirmation. Second, CT-11 asks about visual relevance, which the DOM-only constraint cannot fully settle. The duplicate-image finding is firm and directly verifiable from the URLs; the supporting claims about specific photos depicting notebooks, bookshelves, and an office interior rest on my recognition of those Unsplash IDs rather than on anything I observed on the page, and should be treated as weaker evidence."
  }
}
